WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.900
Retrieval-time authorization is a core security control in permission-aware RAG.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.800
The system should filter candidates using the current caller's permissions before any retrieved text enters the model context.

3
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:20.860
Once sensitive content is placed into the prompt or context window, the system has already crossed an important boundary.

4
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:30.500
The retriever should apply tenant, project, repository, document, classification, and ACL filters based on trusted request context.

5
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:36.120
It should also handle deleted, deprecated, stale, or incomplete records.

6
00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:42.940
If a record lacks the metadata required to make a confident permission decision, deny-by-default behavior is safer than guessing.

7
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.640
Ambiguity should lead to exclusion, not exposure.

8
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.840
Ranking should not reintroduce content that policy already excluded.

9
00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:57.900
In a well-designed pipeline, authorization decisions constrain the candidate set before relevance ranking selects the best allowed results.

10
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.720
This keeps relevance from competing against policy.

11
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:07.460
It also makes the system easier to reason about during review: first decide what is allowed, then decide what is useful.

12
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:13.420
Retrieval logs should support investigation without leaking the content they are meant to protect.

13
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:23.440
Teams may need to know which source identifiers were allowed, which policy checks applied, and why a result was excluded, but they do not always need raw chunk text in logs.

14
00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:30.980
Permission checks should be tested like core application logic because retrieval is the point where private source material becomes model context.
